\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Eliminating alternate vertices of the four-cycle}
\date{2026-10-17}

\p{In the [alternating four-cycle](fdip-0003), take #{t,m>0} and
#{Q=Q_{t,m}=L_t+m^2I}. Its counting-pairing energy is
##{\begin{aligned}
&\langle\phi,Q\phi\rangle\\
&\quad=(\phi_1-\phi_0)^2+t(\phi_2-\phi_1)^2\\
&\qquad+(\phi_3-\phi_2)^2+t(\phi_0-\phi_3)^2\\
&\qquad+m^2\sum_v\phi_v^2.
\end{aligned}}
It is positive for every nonzero field. Retain #{R=(0,2)} and eliminate #{U=(1,3)},
with coordinates #{x=(\phi_0,\phi_2)} and #{y=(\phi_1,\phi_3)} in
these orders. The maps #{x\mapsto x_0\delta_0+x_2\delta_2}
and #{y\mapsto y_1\delta_1+y_3\delta_3} are isometries from
ordinary #{\mathbb R^2}; their sum is the specified orthogonal
splitting, and [product volume](fdip-002J) is #{dx\,dy}.
Write #{a=m^2} and #{s=1+t+a}. Reordering the
[explicit Laplacian](fdip-0008) gives
##{\begin{aligned}
A=C&=sI_2,\\
B=H&=-\begin{pmatrix}1&t\\t&1\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}}
The [elimination theorem](fdip-000D) therefore gives
##{\begin{aligned}
S&=\begin{pmatrix}\alpha&-\beta\\-\beta&\alpha\end{pmatrix},\\
\alpha&=s-\frac{1+t^2}{s},\qquad
\beta=\frac{2t}{s}>0.
\end{aligned}}
Indeed #{HH^T} has diagonal #{1+t^2} and off-diagonal #{2t}, so
#{S=sI_2-s^{-1}HH^T}.}

\p{\citet{Appendix A.5.5}{boyd2004convex}, pp. 650-651, gives the
general \vocabk{Schur complement}{fdip-000D}. Here the chosen blocks make its entries
explicit.}

\tikzfigs{0.55}{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  kept/.style={circle,draw,thick,minimum size=7mm,inner sep=0pt},
  removed/.style={circle,draw,dashed,minimum size=7mm,inner sep=0pt},
  edge/.style={thick}
]
\node[kept] (v0) at (0,3) {$0$};
\node[removed] (v1) at (3.2,3) {$1$};
\node[kept] (v2) at (3.2,0.8) {$2$};
\node[removed] (v3) at (0,0.8) {$3$};
\draw[edge] (v0) -- node[above=2mm] {$1$} (v1);
\draw[edge] (v1) -- node[right=2mm] {$t$} (v2);
\draw[edge] (v2) -- node[below=2mm] {$1$} (v3);
\draw[edge] (v3) -- node[left=2mm] {$t$} (v0);
\draw[-{Stealth[length=2mm]}] (1.6,0.1) -- (1.6,-0.6);
\node[right=2mm] at (1.6,-0.25) {integrate $1,3$};
\node[kept] (r0) at (0,-1.5) {$0$};
\node[kept] (r2) at (3.2,-1.5) {$2$};
\draw[edge] (r0) -- node[above=2mm] {$\beta=2t/s$} (r2);
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{Solid circles are retained; dashed circles are integrated out.
Orientations are suppressed because the quadratic energy is
[orientation independent](fdip-0008). The bottom edge represents
the induced coupling #{\beta}; its precision also has the diagonal
term computed below.}

\p{The vectors #{(1,1)^T/\sqrt2} and #{(1,-1)^T/\sqrt2} diagonalize
#{S}. Their eigenvalues are
##{\begin{aligned}
\sigma_+&=\alpha-\beta
 =\frac{s^2-(1+t)^2}{s}\\
 &=\frac{a(a+2+2t)}{s},\\
\sigma_-&=\alpha+\beta
 =\frac{s^2-(1-t)^2}{s}\\
 &=\frac{(a+2)(a+2t)}{s}.
\end{aligned}}
Both are positive. The factorizations follow from #{s=1+t+a};
they also check positivity without appealing again to the general
theorem. Put
##{\mathcal D=a(a+2)(a+2t)(a+2+2t).}
Then
##{\begin{aligned}
\det C&=s^2,\qquad \det S=\mathcal D/s^2,\\
\det Q&=\det C\det S=\mathcal D,\\
c_C&=\frac{2\pi}{s},\qquad
Z_S=\frac{2\pi s}{\sqrt{\mathcal D}},\\
Z_Q&=c_CZ_S=\frac{(2\pi)^2}{\sqrt{\mathcal D}}.
\end{aligned}}
This reproduces the [four-mode determinant and partition function](fdip-0037).
The \vocabk{effective action}{fdip-000D} is
##{\mathcal A_{\mathrm{eff}}(x)=\tfrac12x^TSx+\log\frac{s}{2\pi}.}
Its constant records the integrated block.}

\p{A retained observable checks more than normalization. For
#{F(x)=x_0x_2}, polynomial integrability and the
[marginal identity](fdip-000E) give
##{\begin{aligned}
\langle\phi_0\phi_2\rangle_Q
 &=\langle x_0x_2\rangle_S
  =(S^{-1})_{02}\\
 &=\frac{\beta}{\alpha^2-\beta^2}
  =\frac{2ts}{\mathcal D}.
\end{aligned}}
The indices #{0,2} here name retained vertices. Inverting the displayed
two-by-two matrix gives its off-diagonal entry
#{\frac{\beta}{\det S}}, which proves the last equalities. Independently, the
[four-cycle spectral table](fdip-0037) gives
##{\begin{aligned}
4\langle\phi_0\phi_2\rangle_Q
 &=\frac1a-\frac1{a+2}\\
 &\quad-\frac1{a+2t}+\frac1{a+2+2t}\\
 &=\frac{8ts}{\mathcal D}.
\end{aligned}}
For the last equality, pair the first two terms and the last two:
their difference has numerator
#{2((a+2t)(a+2+2t)-a(a+2))=8ts} over #{\mathcal D}.
At #{t=m=1}, one obtains
##{\begin{aligned}
S&=\begin{pmatrix}7/3&-2/3\\-2/3&7/3\end{pmatrix},\\
S^{-1}&=\frac1{15}\begin{pmatrix}7&2\\2&7\end{pmatrix},\\
\langle\phi_0\phi_2\rangle_Q&=\frac{2}{15},\\
c_C&=\frac{2\pi}{3},\quad Z_S=\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt5},\quad
Z_Q=\frac{4\pi^2}{3\sqrt5}.
\end{aligned}}
[Fixing the eliminated fields to zero](fdip-003A) instead gives
precision #{A=3I_2}, covariance #{I_2/3} and zero off-diagonal
covariance. At #{t=2,m=1}, the same formulas give
##{\begin{aligned}
S&=\begin{pmatrix}11/4&-1\\-1&11/4\end{pmatrix},\\
\det S&=105/16,\\
S^{-1}&=\frac1{105}\begin{pmatrix}44&16\\16&44\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}}
These are the parameters used in the [symmetry comparison](fdip-000K).}

\p{In the orthonormal sector coordinates
#{x_\pm=(x_0\pm x_2)/\sqrt2} and
#{y_\pm=(y_1\pm y_3)/\sqrt2}, the couplings are
#{-(1+t)} and #{-(1-t)}. Their two eliminated pivots are both
#{s}; each contributes #{\sqrt{2\pi/s}}, whose product is
#{2\pi/s}. The factors #{1/\sqrt2} make these changes orthogonal,
so there is no extra volume factor. The retained variance is
#{\frac12(\sigma_+^{-1}+\sigma_-^{-1})}, and the opposite
covariance is #{\frac12(\sigma_+^{-1}-\sigma_-^{-1})}.
This agrees with the [sector calculation](fdip-000M).}

\p{There was no edge between vertices #{0} and #{2} in the supplied
cycle. Elimination induces one in the effective quadratic form:
##{S=\beta\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\-1&1\end{pmatrix}
       +\sigma_+I_2.}
Thus #{\beta} is an effective edge weight and #{\sigma_+} an effective
mass coefficient. This is a finite instance of fill-in: eliminating
a single vertex joined to retained vertices with weights #{w_i}
and diagonal precision #{c>0} subtracts #{ww^T/c}, creating
off-diagonal entries #{-w_iw_j/c} where no retained edge existed.
The formula is the same Schur complement with #{H=-w} and #{C=(c)}.
Locality in the original graph need not survive elimination.}

\p{The half-turn preserves both sets #{R,U} and swaps the two
coordinates within each. For the swap matrix #{J}, the displayed
#{S} satisfies #{JSJ=S}; the induced two-site form retains this
symmetry. The density and orthogonal-volume invariance then show
that the retained law is invariant as well.}

\p{At #{m=0}, #{C=(1+t)I_2} remains positive. Square completion
and integration over the eliminated block still give
##{S=\frac{2t}{1+t}\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\-1&1\end{pmatrix},
\qquad c_C=\frac{2\pi}{1+t}.}
The retained constant mode has zero energy, so its full integral
diverges by the [kernel-box argument](fdip-0039). The finite fiber
factor cannot be divided by a finite retained partition function.}

\p{The [global mean-zero law](fdip-000C) instead uses the space
#{H=\{\phi:\sum_v\phi_v=0\}} with its own induced volume.
Its constraint #{x_0+x_2+y_1+y_3=0} couples the two parts;
#{H} is neither their unrestricted product nor the product of their
separate mean-zero lines. At #{t=1}, write a field in the
[three positive orthonormal modes](fdip-0038) as
#{\phi=z_1u_1+z_2u_2+z_3u_3}. The coordinates are independent
centered Gaussians of precisions #{2,2,4}. Put
##{r=\frac{z_1+z_2}{\sqrt2},\qquad
v=\frac{z_1-z_2}{\sqrt2}.}
This orthogonal change preserves #{z_1^2+z_2^2=r^2+v^2} and
two-dimensional volume. Thus #{r,v,z_3} still have independent
centered Gaussian laws of precisions #{2,2,4}. The retained unit
coordinates satisfy
##{\begin{aligned}
x_-&=(\phi_0-\phi_2)/\sqrt2=r,\\
x_+&=(\phi_0+\phi_2)/\sqrt2=z_3/\sqrt2.
\end{aligned}}
Integrating the normalized #{v} factor and changing variable
#{z_3=\sqrt2x_+} give independent retained Gaussian densities
of precisions #{2} and #{8}. This density calculation identifies
the law, not merely its covariance. Returning orthogonally to
#{(x_0,x_2)} gives
##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Cov}(x)&=\frac1{16}
  \begin{pmatrix}5&-3\\-3&5\end{pmatrix},\\
Q_{H\to R}&=\begin{pmatrix}5&3\\3&5\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}}
These matrices multiply to the identity. The projected normalizer
for this precision is #{\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{16}}=\frac\pi2}.}

\p{In contrast, directly setting #{m=0,t=1} in the Schur formula
gives the singular precision
#{\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\-1&1\end{pmatrix}}.
Separately constraining that retained model to #{x_0+x_2=0}
leaves unit direction #{(1,-1)/\sqrt2} with precision #{2},
normalizer #{\sqrt\pi} and ambient covariance
#{\frac14\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\-1&1\end{pmatrix}}.
These are three distinct specified measures. The
[pinned affine law](fdip-0035) is another constraint with its own
induced volume; fixing a vertex is not the marginal operation above.}

\p{The [arithmetic pivot boundary](fdip-000U) is separate from
the positive-real case. At #{m^2=1,t=-2}, #{s=t+2=0}, whereas
#{\det Q=1\cdot3\cdot(-3)\cdot(-1)=9}. Thus the full map can
be invertible while this eliminated pivot is not. At #{t=-1},
the pivot exists but the later [ratio denominator](fdip-000W)
#{t+1} vanishes. Neither value satisfies the positive-weight
hypotheses used for the Gaussian laws here.}
